'\" t
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.\" Manual page for the accudisc command-line tool.
.\" Requires the tbl(1) preprocessor (see the '\" t line above).
.TH ACCUDISC 1 "2026-08-10" "AccuDisc 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
accudisc \- precision Red Book CD\-DA reader and writer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B accudisc
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR DEV ]
.RB [ \-\-driver
.IR auto | NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-drivers\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B accudisc
.BR \-\-help | \-h
.br
.B accudisc
.BR \-\-version | \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B accudisc
is the command-line front end to
.BR accudisc (8),
a low-level Red Book CD\-DA capture and recording library. It reads audio CDs
\(em and the audio portion of Mixed Mode discs \(em together with the full range
of data and metadata the format carries: TOC and session structure, the P\(enW
subchannel, C2 error pointers, CD\-Text, ISRC, MCN, pregaps and indices. It also
burns a single audio session Disc\-At\-Once to CD\-R/RW.
.PP
.B accudisc
moves bits and reports what it saw. It performs no post-processing: no
tagging, no encoding, no database lookups (CDDB, MusicBrainz), no audio
analysis. That is a deliberate scope boundary; those tasks belong to the
calling application.
.PP
The tool is designed to be driven by a front-end application as much as by a
person. Machine-readable output \(em the token lines on stdout, the
.B \-\-progress\-fd
channel, the
.B \-\-map\-file
status map, the
.B \-\-subq\-map\-file
Q health map and the exit codes \(em is a stable contract, specified in
.IR docs/reference/cli\-machine\-interface.md .
Human-readable stderr output (the progress line, the summary block, log
messages) is
.B not
stable and must not be parsed.
.SS Global options
These are accepted anywhere on the command line; the first bare word is the
command and everything else is passed to it.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " DEV"
Optical device to operate on. Default
.IR /dev/sr0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-driver " auto" | NAME
Permit loading a vendor driver.
.I auto
matches the driver to the identified drive;
.I NAME
requests a specific one (for example
.IR plextor ).
Without this option no vendor opcode is ever issued. A missing or
non-matching driver is not fatal: a warning is logged and the device stays on
generic MMC/SG. Because vendor opcodes need the kernel's full SG_IO command
set, passing this option opens the device read-write.
.TP
.BI \-\-drivers\-dir " DIR"
Where to look for driver modules. Default:
.BR $ACCUDISC_DRIVER_DIR ,
falling back to the installed driver directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the usage text on stdout and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the library version and exit 0. Equivalent to the
.B version
command.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS info
Identify the drive (INQUIRY). Prints
.IR vendor ", " product ", " revision ,
the manufacturing
.I read_offset
in samples from the built-in offset table (or
.IR unknown ),
and the
.I access
method in use \(em
.RI \(lq "generic MMC" \(rq
or the attached driver's name and description.
.SS offset
Report the drive's read offset from the compiled table. With
.BI \-\-vendor " V"
and
.BI \-\-product " P"
the answer comes from the table alone and
.B no drive is opened
\(em the table is knowledge about drives, so a caller holding captures from
hardware that is not present can still ask about it.
.PP
AccuDisc reports the offset and never applies it. Correct once, at storage: a
library that could apply would give every consumer a second site where the shift
might happen, and a double correction is silent \(em well-formed audio, wrong by
twice the offset.
.PP
The unit is a
.B sample
= one stereo frame = 4 bytes, 588 per sector; this is the unit REDUMP and
AccurateRip both use, and the reason the splice arithmetic is
.IR "track_start * 2352 + read_offset * 4" .
A positive offset means the drive reads early.
.I read_offset
is one
.B signed
integer; there is no separate sign field, because two representations of one
fact can disagree.
.PP
Exit 3 when no source holds the drive, and also when the sources
.B disagree
\(em then
.I read_offset
is
.IR unknown ,
followed by
.BI conflict " N"
and one
.I value
line per candidate with its sources. Nothing here picks between them: an offset
applied wrongly is silent, so the choice is the caller's and must be passed back
through the caller's own configuration.
.SS disc
Pre-flight guard: classify the loaded disc as rippable audio, a blank to burn,
or neither. One token line on stdout:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
disc kind=<BLANK|AUDIO|NEITHER> profile=0x<nnnn> \\
     disc_status=<0|1|2|\-1> erasable=<0|1|\-1> \\
     audio_tracks=<n> data_tracks=<n> reason=<slug> [tray=<state>]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I profile
is the MMC current-profile code (0x08 CD\-ROM, 0x09 CD\-R, 0x0A CD\-RW);
.I disc_status
is 0 empty / 1 incomplete / 2 complete, and
.I erasable
is 1 for CD\-RW \(em both are
.B \-1
when not obtainable, never silently 0.
.I tray
is emitted only with
.IR reason=no_medium .
Exit 3 means the disc was classified successfully but neither ripping nor
burning is legal for it. Parse tokens, not positions; new keys may be
appended.
.SS toc
List tracks. Prefers READ TOC format 2 (the lead-in, which carries session
structure) and degrades automatically to format 0 on an unreadable lead-in,
reporting which path answered on the final
.I source=
/
.I degrade=
line. A degrade does
.B not
change the exit code: this command promises track geometry and a degrade still
delivers it in full.
.SS fulltoc [\fIFILE\fR]
With no argument, print the parsed session structure (sessions, first/last
track, lead-out, per-track LBA and CTRL). With
.IR FILE ,
dump the raw READ TOC format 2 response to that file instead.
.SS cdtext \fIFILE\fR
Dump the raw CD\-Text packs (READ TOC format 5) to
.IR FILE .
.I FILE
is mandatory; without it the command is not recognised. If the disc carries no
CD\-Text, no file is written and the exit code is 3.
.SS text
Decode and print CD\-Text from block 0: album title, performer and UPC, then
per-track title, performer, songwriter and ISRC. Only fields actually present
are printed.
.SS scan
Read the MCN and the per-track ISRCs from the Q subchannel. Prints
.RI \(lq mcn\ <n> \(rq
or
.RI \(lq "mcn absent" \(rq,
then one line per audio track. Exit 3 when neither an MCN nor any ISRC was
found.
.SS pregaps
Per-track index and pregap map decoded from the Q subchannel, CRC-gated. One
row per track boundary reporting
.IR pregap\ Nf ,
.I gapless
or
.IR UNKNOWN ,
with the CRC-ok/bad counts behind the verdict, and a
.RI \(lq [!]\ Q\ index1 \(rq
note where the subchannel disagrees with the TOC. Exit 3 when at least one
boundary decoded as
.IR UNKNOWN .
A boundary whose
.I read
fails ends the scan with exit 2 \(em but the rows decoded before it are still
printed, and stdout is flushed ahead of the stderr diagnostic. This is the one
command where a nonzero exit can carry usable stdout, so treat the row count
as a floor rather than a total.
.SS features [\fIFLAG\fR]
Probe drive capability. Flags may be combined; a bare invocation is equivalent
to
.BR \-\-all .
.RS 4
.TP
.B \-\-c2
The C2 boolean question. Prints the CD Read feature descriptor, one
.RI \(lq combo\ <name>\ ok|failed \(rq
line for each of
.IR c2 ,
.IR sub_raw ,
.IR sub_q ,
.I c2+sub_raw
and
.IR c2+sub_q ,
and a
.I verdict
of
.BR C2_SUPPORTED ,
.B C2_UNVERIFIED
or
.BR C2_UNSUPPORTED .
This is the only flag that gates the exit code.
.TP
.B \-\-stream
Accurate Stream / positional determinism, probed mid-disc. Prints
.IR "accurate_stream yes|no|unknown" .
Informational.
.TP
.B \-\-rotation
Drive rotation type (CLV, CAV, P\-CAV, Z\-CLV) classified from the nominal
RPM-derived performance curve. Disc-independent, so it answers
.RI \(lq "is this a CAV drive" \(rq
and not
.RI \(lq "what will it do with this disc" \(rq;
it deliberately does not print the current speed.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Everything above, informational, exit 0.
.RE
.SS speed [\fIX\fR]
Report the drive's read-speed state: the mode page 2A maximum and current
speeds, the rotation classification, and each nominal performance curve
segment. With
.IR X ,
set an
.RI N x
ceiling first, preferring SET STREAMING and falling back to SET CD SPEED.
.PP
A drive honours only the rungs its firmware implements, and snaps anything
else DOWN to the next one it has \(em on a PX\-716A,
.B speed 16
adopts 8\(mux. When that happens an extra line is printed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quantized  asked 16x, drive adopted 8x \(em this drive's ladder has no 16x rung
.EE
.RE
.PP
The line is absent when the request is honoured, so its presence is the
signal. See
.B speeds
for the whole ladder in advance, including which settings are real rungs.
.RS 4
.TP
.B \-\-exact
Pin the rate rather than setting a ceiling (CLV). Requires
.IR X .
The drive may refuse; an Illegal Request here is the answer, and is reported
as such before the command exits nonzero.
.TP
.BI \-\-start " LBA"
.TQ
.BI \-\-count " N"
Request the ceiling for that LBA range only. Requires
.IR X ;
.B \-\-count
requires
.BR \-\-start .
Locality is drive-dependent \(em a performance hint, honoured whole-disc on
every drive tested so far. These three options route through SET STREAMING
only, with no SET CD SPEED fallback.
.RE
.PP
A bare
.B speed
.I X
is a request for that end state and therefore
.BR persists ;
it is not restored on exit.
.SS speed\-uncap [on|off]
Report or set the vendor read-speed uncap (Plextor: SpeedRead), which lifts the
firmware's CD read-speed ceiling. Requires
.BR \-\-driver .
With no argument it reports only. Prints
.PP
.RS 4
speed\-uncap <on|off> max_kbps <n> max_x <n>
.RE
.PP
The mode page 2A ceiling is printed alongside because that is the observable
the setting moves. This is
.B persistent drive state:
it survives the process and the handle, and is only changed by setting it again
or power-cycling the drive. Exit 2 when no attached driver offers the
capability.
.SS media
Identify recordable media from its ATIP. One line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
atip leadin=<mm:ss:ff> leadout=<mm:ss:ff> \\
     type=<CD\-R|CD\-RW> manufacturer=<name>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I leadin
is the ATIP lead-in start, which doubles as the manufacturer identification
code;
.I leadout
is the last-possible lead-out start, i.e. capacity.
.B manufacturer=
is deliberately last because its value may contain spaces \(em parse it as
rest-of-line. It is empty when the code is not in the built-in catalogue. A
disc with no ATIP (a pressed CD, or no recordable medium) prints
.I atip absent
and exits 3.
.SS speeds
Probe which speed settings the drive really honours, by timed streaming reads
at each rung. One line per candidate:
.PP
.RS 4
speed req=<x> page2a=<x> measured=<x.xx> [inner= middle= outer=] [min= max=]
.RE
.PP
.I measured
is ground truth;
.I page2a
is shown so quantization and clamping are visible. Every rung gets its own
cache-fresh window, disjoint from every other. The drive is left at the last
candidate tested.
.PP
By default each rung is timed three times \(em once in each third of the disc
\(em and reported as
.IR inner ", " middle " and " outer ,
because on a CAV drive the rate is radius-dependent and a single figure carries
a radius term it cannot declare.
.I measured
is the
.I middle
band, the same quantity it has always been. See
.B \-\-quick
for the single-band probe.
.PP
.I min
and
.I max
are the slowest and fastest of the three. They are
.B order statistics, not locations,
and they are printed alongside the named bands rather than in place of them
because the difference is only invisible while the curve rises monotonically
with radius \(em which is the ordinary case and therefore the one that would
hide a mix-up. When a governor step part-way across the disc, a cache-served
band, or another process on the bus disturbs a rung,
.I min
is no longer
.IR inner ,
and that disagreement is itself the finding.
.PP
Because the rungs are laid out along the probed span, a comparison
.I between
rungs carries a radius term: with the default descending ladder the fast rungs
are measured innermost, which biases against them. Treat a modest cross-rung
inversion as unproven rather than measured.
.PP
A rung normally reports
.B below
its own requested speed, and that is geometry rather than a fault. Under CAV the
rate rises with radius, so an advertised
.RI \(dq N x\(dq
is the rate at an outer radius the drive chooses \(em on the PX\-716 the vendor
tool documents it as address
.BR 68:00:00 ,
near the edge of a full-length disc. A disc whose lead-out falls short of that
address cannot reach
.I N
at any setting or in any band, however healthy it is. Judge a rung by the shape
of its bands against the length of the disc, not by the gap between
.I outer
and
.IR req .
.RS 4
.TP
.BI \-\-start " LBA"
Where to probe. Default: 0, or one quarter of the way into the disc under
.BR \-\-quick .
Narrowing the span makes the bands thirds of whatever was probed rather than
of the disc, which is noted on stderr \(em three bands of the last sixth of a
disc are still three well-formed numbers with nothing to mark them as local.
.TP
.BI \-\-ladder " LIST"
Comma-separated candidate speeds, at most 16, e.g.
.IR 32,16,8,4 .
Default: 52,48,40,32,24,16,8,4 filtered to those at or below the drive's page
2A maximum.
.TP
.B \-\-quick
Time each rung once, in the middle half of the disc, instead of once per third.
Three times faster, and it reports
.I measured
alone: no
.IR inner / middle / outer ,
no
.IR min / max ,
no verdicts and no admitted ladder. This was the default before 0.9.0.
.B \-\-sweep
is still accepted and now selects the default, which is the sweep.
.IP
A probe that cannot fit three bands per rung in the span falls back to this
automatically and says so, since the loss is visible in the output. Asking for
the sweep explicitly is refused instead of narrowed.
.PP
Unlike the cross-rung comparison above, the spread
.I within
a rung is sound: the three bands are equally spaced for every rung, the timed
length is identical across them, and one speed setting covers all three. On a
CAV rung that spread is the CAV curve itself, so a rung that comes out flat is
either clamped to CLV or is not being measured properly. Absent
.I min
and
.I max
on a line mean no gradient was obtained for that rung, which is not the same as
a gradient of zero \(em and unlike them, a single band figure stands alone, so
a band that failed drops only its own token.
.PP
The sweep also decides which settings are
.I real
rungs, appending
.I verdict=admitted
,
.IR verdict=duplicate: N
or
.IR verdict=quantized: N
per line and a final
.RI "\fBladder admitted=\fP" x , x ,...
line.
.I quantized
means the drive itself reported a lower speed than requested;
.I duplicate
means the rung measured no faster than a lower admitted one once the radius
term is discounted;
.I admitted
means it measured materially faster. Verdicts hold for this disc, not this
drive \(em probe each disc. Report-only: every candidate is still listed and
AccuDisc never rewrites a caller's ladder from it.
.RE
.SS c2lag
Probe the drive's C2-bitmap-to-audio alignment. Report-only: AccuDisc never
applies the lag to delivered bitmaps. On success:
.PP
.RS 4
c2lag pairs=<L> peak=<milli> runner=<milli> flags=<n> diffs=<n> active=<n>
.RE
.PP
.I pairs
is the lag in sample pairs; a fired bit at bitmap position
.I i
describes audio byte
.IR i\ \-\ 4*pairs .
Point this at a
.B damaged
span \(em C2 must actually fire for the measurement to exist. Exit 3 means
inconclusive, and the stderr message distinguishes
.RI \(lq "no C2 fired in the span" \(rq
from
.RI \(lq "C2 seen but the evidence is too thin" \(rq.
.RS 4
.TP
.BI \-\-start " LBA"
First sector. Default 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-count " N"
Sectors to scan. Default: to the lead-out.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " X"
Set the read speed first. A higher speed can be what makes marginal defects
fire C2 at all.
.RE
.SS stop
Spin the spindle down. Does not eject.
.SS eject
Open the tray / unload the disc.
.SS load
Close the tray / load the disc.
.SS read
Read CD\-DA sectors. See
.B READ OPTIONS
below.
.SS write
Burn one audio session Disc\-At\-Once from a cdrdao
.I .toc
plus the raw audio BIN it names. Requires a blank disc, and opens the device
read-write. Prints
.I write done sectors=<n> mode=<simulate|burn>
on completion, with
.I caveats=1
appended when the burn completed but a caveat was logged.
.RS 4
.TP
.BI \-\-toc " FILE"
The cdrdao
.I .toc
describing the layout. Mandatory.
.TP
.BI \-\-bin " FILE"
The raw s16le audio image it references, 2352 bytes per sector \(em exactly the
read path's
.B \-\-pcm
output. Mandatory.
.TP
.BI \-\-cdtext " FILE"
A raw READ TOC format-0x05 blob, byte-for-byte as
.B read \-\-cdtext
emits it, laid into the lead-in verbatim.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Test-write: run the whole path with the laser off.
.TP
.B \-\-byteswap
Swap each 16-bit audio sample before writing. Audio byte order is
drive-specific; settle it empirically by read-back before trusting a real burn.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " X"
Write speed. 0 or absent leaves the drive's current setting.
.TP
.BI \-\-progress\-fd " N"
Machine progress and summary on fd
.IR N ;
see
.B MACHINE OUTPUT
below.
.RE
.SS write\-offset
Measure how far early or late a drive
.B burns
audio. Unlike the read offset this cannot be looked up: no live source
publishes per\-drive write offsets, so it is obtained by burning a known signal
and reading it back. This subcommand supplies the two ends of that procedure
and needs no device; the burn and the read\-back in between are the ordinary
.B write
and
.B read \-\-pcm
commands.
.RS 4
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " FILE"
Write the 75\-second test signal here as raw s16le PCM: digital silence with a
one\-frame noise burst at 1\ s and another at 60\ s. Deterministic \(em the same
bytes on every machine, so a disc can be re\-measured later and compared.
.TP
.BI \-\-toc " FILE"
With
.BR \-\-signal ,
also write a matching cdrdao
.I .toc
naming it, ready to hand to
.BR write .
.TP
.BI \-\-measure " FILE"
Locate both pulses in a read\-back and report the write offset.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-offset " N"
The
.B reading
drive's offset in samples.
.B Mandatory,
and deliberately not defaulted: what comes off the disc carries the read and
write offsets summed, so a defaulted zero would return a confident number wrong
by exactly the drive's read offset \(em and zero is a legitimate read offset for
hundreds of real drives, so nothing downstream could tell.
.RE
.PP
Two pulses are measured, not one, so that a defective disc can be told from a
real offset. If they disagree the command prints
.I write_offset unknown
with both per\-pulse results and exits 3; it never averages them, because the
disagreement is a property of that disc rather than of the drive. Sign
convention matches the read offset: positive means the drive burns
.BR late ,
so correcting a burn means shifting the source by \-W.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
accudisc write\-offset \-\-signal w.pcm \-\-toc w.toc
accudisc \-\-device /dev/sr0 write \-\-toc w.toc \-\-bin w.pcm
accudisc \-\-device /dev/sr0 read \-\-pcm back.pcm
accudisc write\-offset \-\-measure back.pcm \-\-read\-offset 30
.EE
.RE
.SS cxscan
Hardware C1/C2/CU error census, using the drive's own error counters (Plextor:
Q\-Check). Requires
.BR \-\-driver .
The TOC is read before the counters are armed, so lead-in traffic never lands
in the first sample's interval. One line per sample on stdout:
.PP
.RS 4
cx <lba> <c1> <c2> <cu>
.RE
.PP
followed by a totals-and-peaks summary on stderr. The default sample cadence is
75 sectors \(em one second of audio, which is what makes the figures comparable
with other C1/C2 tools. A failed read does not stop the census; it is reported
on stderr and the scan continues. Exit 2 when no attached driver offers the
capability.
.RS 4
.TP
.BI \-\-start " LBA"
First sector. Default 0. The scan always runs to the lead-out.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " X"
Set the read speed before scanning.
.RE
.PP
Note that
.B \-\-start
and
.B \-\-speed
are accepted here although the built-in usage text does not list them.
.SS version
Print the library version and exit 0.
.SH READ OPTIONS
Options to the
.B read
command.
.SS Range selection
.B read
reads the
.B audio tracks of one session
\(em not the whole disc and not the whole session. With none of the options
below it resolves the session automatically: the single session containing
audio, or exit 1 with the sessions listed if more than one does. The range then
runs from the first audio track's start (including its pregap, so on session 1
it begins at LBA 0 and captures hidden-track-one audio) to the end of the last.
.TP
.BI \-\-start " LBA"
First sector. Default: the start of the audio session.
.TP
.BI \-\-count " N"
Sectors to read. Default: to that session's lead-out.
.TP
.BI \-\-session " N"
Which session to read. Required when more than one session has audio.
.TP
.BI \-\-track " N"
.TQ
.BI \-\-tracks " A\-B"
Read track
.I N
alone, or tracks
.I A
through
.I B
inclusive. Both spellings accept both forms. Selection is by track
.B number,
not index, and overrides
.BR \-\-session .
Both tracks must be in the same session: a span across a session seam would
include the lead-out and lead-in between them, and is refused with exit 1.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Bypass the audio-range guard \(em data tracks, session seams, untrusted TOC
geometry. The drive will still reject what it cannot read. Deliberately
separate from
.BR \-\-any .
.SS Capture content
.TP
.B \-\-no\-c2
Do not request C2 error pointers. They are requested by default and ride the
same READ CD transfer as the audio, so they are effectively free.
.TP
.B \-\-c2beb
Request the 296-byte C2 + block-error-bits variant instead of the 294-byte
pointer bitmap.
.TP
.BI \-\-sub " raw" | q
Also capture the subchannel: 96-byte raw interleaved P\(enW, or the drive's
16-byte formatted Q block.
.TP
.B \-\-any
Set the READ CD expected sector type to ANY rather than CD\-DA. This selects
the command's type field only; it is not an override of the range guard.
.SS Output files
.TP
.BI \-\-pcm " FILE"
Raw s16le PCM, 2352 bytes per sector, no offset correction applied.
.TP
.BI \-\-c2f " FILE"
The C2 bitmap stream, passed through from the drive untouched. Requires C2, so
it conflicts with
.BR \-\-no\-c2 .
.TP
.BI \-\-subf " FILE"
The subchannel stream. Requires
.BR \-\-sub .
.TP
.BI \-\-cdg " FILE"
CD+G packs: the R\(enW channels de-interleaved and Reed\-Solomon corrected, 24
bytes per pack, which is the
.I .cdg
file format. Requires
.BR "\-\-sub raw" ,
since the formatted Q form has already discarded those channels. Pack count is
.IR 4*sectors\ \-\ 7 ;
the convolutional de-interleave has an 8-pack span, so the loss lands at the
tail of the stream and emitted pack
.I k
is logical pack
.IR k .
An ordinary audio CD yields all MODE 0 (no R\(enW data recorded), and the
summary says so rather than leaving zero graphics packs to look like a decoder
fault.
.TP
.BI \-\-fulltoc " FILE"
Also dump the raw full TOC in the same invocation, so a complete capture is one
spin-up rather than three.
.TP
.BI \-\-cdtext " FILE"
Also dump the raw CD\-Text packs. Absence is noted on stderr and no file is
written; it does not affect the read's exit code.
.PP
Hard-unreadable sectors are zero-filled in every stream (PCM 0, C2 all-ones,
SUB 0), so all outputs stay exactly
.I count
sectors and never desync.
.SS Accuracy and recovery
All of these are off by default; with none of them set,
.B read
is a single-pass streaming capture.
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk " N"
Sectors per READ CD command. Default: the maximum that fits under 64 KiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " K"
Per-sector attempts after a chunk read fails. Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-c2\-retries " N"
Hunt a C2-clean copy of each flagged sector with up to
.I N
cache-defeated rereads. The best read wins and the whole sector is replaced, so
audio, C2 and subchannel stay single-read aligned. Requires C2. Default 0 (off).
.TP
.BI \-\-verify " P"
Read everything
.I P
times with cache defeat and compare. Sectors whose reads disagree are resolved
by consensus (any two identical independent reads) or delivered best-effort and
marked SUSPECT. Default 1 (off).
.TP
.BI \-\-overlap " K"
Boundary overlap check: extend each chunk by
.I K
trailing sectors and verify the seam against the next chunk's head. Catches
drive slips at chunk boundaries that back-to-back reads cannot see; mismatches
go to consensus. Default 0 (off), clamped to 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-ladder " LIST"
Speed ladder for problem-sector rereads, at most 8 rungs, e.g.
.IR 32,16,8,4 .
Rescue attempt
.I n
runs at the
.IR n -th
rung, descending toward slow careful reads; the pass speed is restored before
the next streaming chunk. Pick rungs that differ from
.BR \-\-speed :
consensus votes must be speed-diverse, because a drive can misread the same way
at the same speed every time. Verify passes themselves stream at the pass speed
\(em drives recalibrate on every speed change, so per-chunk switching thrashes.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " X"
Set the drive read speed to
.RI X x
before reading. Best-effort, and never auto-restored afterwards.
.IP
If the drive snaps the request down to a lower rung \(em
.RI 16 x
becomes 8\(mux on a PX\-716A \(em a notice is written to stderr and the read
proceeds at the lower rate.
.B \-q
does not suppress it: a read running at half the requested speed is a
data\-integrity notice, and quiet means no human is watching. Use
.B speeds
to learn which settings are real rungs before choosing one.
.TP
.B \-\-uncap
Lift the vendor read-speed cap for this read only, restoring the drive's prior
setting on every exit path. Requires
.BR \-\-driver .
.PP
This does
.B not
speed up an audio read. The drive governs CD\-DA to 40\(mux regardless of the
uncap, and mode page 2A reports the request rather than the governed rate, so
the 48\(mux it exposes is not reachable on an audio disc.
.PP
Releases before 0.6.0 refused
.B \-\-uncap
together with
.BR \-\-sub ,
and warned when the uncap merely appeared to be on. Both are gone: the state
they defended against is not one the hardware can enter. The combination is now
ordinary, and neither refused nor warned about.
.SS Progress and status
.TP
.B \-\-map
Render a condensed per-sector disc map on stderr when the read finishes. Each
cell is the worst state in its bucket:
.B .
ok,
.B r
recovered,
.B !
C2,
.B ?
suspect,
.B X
hard,
.RB \(lq \ \(rq
pending.
.TP
.BI \-\-map\-file " F"
Live status map.
.I F
is created and truncated to exactly
.I count
bytes, one status byte per sector in span order (byte
.I i
is LBA
.IR start+i );
there is no header. The engine updates the bytes in place through a
.B MAP_SHARED
mapping, so another process can
.BR mmap (2)
the file read-only and watch the rip live. The file persists after exit for
post-mortem reads. See
.B STATUS MAP ENCODING
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-subq\-map\-file " F"
Live Q-subchannel health map, in every mechanical respect the same as
.B \-\-map\-file
\(em same size, same ordering, same
.B MAP_SHARED
update, same persistence \(em but a separate lane holding a different
measurement. Requires
.BR "\-\-sub raw" ;
anything else is refused rather than producing a map with nothing in it. See
.B Q HEALTH MAP ENCODING
below.
.IP
It is not derivable from the status map and neither substitutes for the other:
the audio and the Q subchannel of a sector fail independently, so a sector can
be audio-clean with a dead Q (a lost pregap or index) or the reverse. Measured
on an ordinary audio disc: 3000 sectors, zero C2 and zero hard errors in the
status lane, 32 CRC-failed Q frames in this one.
.TP
.BI \-\-progress\-fd " N"
Machine-readable progress and summary on caller-supplied fd
.IR N .
Not muted by
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: suppress the human progress line, the resolved-range echo and the CD+G
summary. The machine channel is unaffected.
.SH STATUS MAP ENCODING
One byte per sector. The low nibble is the state, the high nibble a severity;
the encoding is the public ABI in
.I accudisc.h
and is read with the
.B ACCUDISC_MAP_STATE()
and
.B ACCUDISC_MAP_SEVERITY()
macros.
.TS
lb lb lb
l l l.
value	name	meaning
0x0	ACCUDISC_MAP_PENDING	not yet attempted
0x1	ACCUDISC_MAP_OK	read clean
0x2	ACCUDISC_MAP_C2	delivered with fired C2 pointer(s)
0x3	ACCUDISC_MAP_HARD	unreadable \(em zero-filled in the output
0x4	ACCUDISC_MAP_RECOVERED	problem seen, clean/agreeing copy won
0x5	ACCUDISC_MAP_SUSPECT	reads disagree \(em best-effort delivered
.TE
.PP
The severity nibble carries approximately log2 of the fired C2 bit count for
.BR C2 ,
the number of extra reads it took for
.BR RECOVERED ,
approximately log2 of the disagreeing byte count for
.BR SUSPECT ,
and 0 otherwise.
.SH Q HEALTH MAP ENCODING
One byte per sector, written by
.BR \-\-subq\-map\-file .
Same shape as the status map above and read with
.B ACCUDISC_SUBQ_STATE()
\(em but a
.B separate vocabulary
that happens to share the numbering, so the two decoders are
.B not
interchangeable. Decoding a Q byte with
.B ACCUDISC_MAP_STATE()
yields a well-formed name for a state that never occurred.
.TS
lb lb lb
l l l.
value	name	meaning
0x0	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_PENDING	not yet attempted
0x1	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_OK	CRC-16 verified, ADR=1 position frame
0x2	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_BAD	CRC-16 failed
0x3	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_NO_POSITION	CRC-16 verified, ADR \(!= 1 (MCN/ISRC)
0x4	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_NO_AUDIO	sector unreadable; no frame delivered
0x5	ACCUDISC_SUBQ_MISPOSITION	valid ADR=1 frame naming a DIFFERENT LBA
.TE
.PP
The severity nibble is always 0: Q integrity is one CRC-16 over one 12-byte
frame, so there is no gradient to report.
.PP
.B MISPOSITION looks healthy and is not.
The frame verified, carries ADR=1 and is internally consistent, so any check
that inspects only the frame calls it OK. What makes it a fault is the
comparison against the sector that was asked for: the drive lost its place and
returned intact audio from the wrong address. Measured on a PX\-716A at maximum
CAV speed \(em 17 consecutive sectors from 2048 sectors earlier, C2 silent, four
independent passes agreeing byte\-for\-byte. Over 1404000 sectors: 17/17 true
positives, zero false positives. It is 0 whenever raw subchannel was not
requested, so zero means nothing without
.BR \-\-sub " " raw .
.PP
.B NO_POSITION is healthy.
MCN and ISRC frames are interleaved into the position stream by the pressing
itself \(em measured at 1.00% of sectors on a disc carrying an MCN, and 0.00%
on a pressing carrying neither. A health figure is therefore
.RB ( OK " + " NO_POSITION )
over the sectors attempted; counting it as damage under a worst-wins
aggregation into cells flags every cell on a flawless disc.
.PP
.B NO_AUDIO is why this map exists.
An unreadable sector is delivered zero-filled, and a zero-filled Q frame
.I fails
CRC-16 rather than being recognised as absent. Anything recomputing this lane
from the delivered subchannel therefore records Q damage that was never on the
disc, on exactly the sectors whose audio is already lost \(em where it
corroborates the real failure instead of standing out. Measured: 16 unreadable
sectors, 16 fabricated CRC failures from the delivered bytes, 16
.B NO_AUDIO
here.
.PP
Every state is a
.B relative
claim \(em stable, clean or unstable across the reads of
.I this run
\(em never verification against the pressing's canonical bytes. A drive that
misreads deterministically passes every relative check. Absolute gates
(AccurateRip, CTDB) are the calling application's job and always outrank
anything recorded here.
.SH MACHINE OUTPUT
Newline-delimited tokens on the fd given to
.BR \-\-progress\-fd ,
unaffected by
.BR \-q .
For
.BR read :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
progress <done> <total>
summary hard=<n> c2=<n> recovered=<n> suspect=<n> rereads=<n> slips=<n> \\
        subq_total=<n> subq_ok=<n> subq_bad=<n>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I progress
lines are throttled to roughly four per second and the final one always reports
.IR "<total> <total>" .
.I summary
is emitted exactly once, after a completed read, clean or degraded; a read that
dies or exits 2 emits none.
.I hard
counts zero-filled unreadable sectors,
.I c2
sectors whose delivered copy still carries fired C2 bits,
.I recovered
and
.I suspect
the consensus outcomes,
.I rereads
extra per-sector reads issued,
.I slips
positioning-slip detections, and the
.I subq_*
triple the Q-frame CRC census over
.B \-\-sub raw
reads. New key=value pairs may be appended; parse the line as tokens, not
positionally.
.PP
For
.BR write :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
progress <done> <total>
summary sectors=<n> mode=<simulate|burn> result=<ok|caveats|not_blank|error>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Unlike
.BR read ,
the
.I write
summary is emitted on
.B every
outcome, so a caller can key on
.I result=
instead of the exit code. That matters because exit 2 covers both
.I not_blank
and other failures and the exit code alone cannot separate them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Completed, no caveats.
.TP
.B 1
Usage, argument, or local file error.
.TP
.B 2
Fatal: the device, the transport, or the command could not complete.
.TP
.B 3
Completed with caveats.
.PP
Exit 3 by command:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.BR read :
the image was delivered in full, but hard errors, suspect sectors, or
C2-flagged sectors remain after recovery. Exit 0 means no
.I relative
signal fired; it is not verification.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cdtext ", " fulltoc :
the drive answered but the disc carries no data of that format, and no output
file was written. A drive that
.I rejects
the request is exit 2 \(em deliberately not conflated with absence.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scan :
neither an MCN nor any ISRC was found.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR pregaps :
at least one track boundary decoded as UNKNOWN.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR c2lag :
the probe ran but was inconclusive.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR media :
the disc carries no ATIP.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR disc :
the disc was classified successfully and
.I kind=NEITHER
\(em refusing is a
.I successful
classification, which is why it is 3 and not 2.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR write :
the burn completed but a caveat was logged \(em today, a CD\-Text SIZE_INFO
pack whose declared track range disagrees with the
.IR .toc .
The disc
.B was
written.
.RE
.PP
Exception:
.B features
keeps its frozen contract \(em exit
.B 0
if and only if C2 is clearly usable (verdict
.BR C2_SUPPORTED ),
1 otherwise, 2 on a probe error. The other
.B features
flags never gate the exit code.
.PP
Note also that
.B write
uses exit
.B 2
for a disc that is not blank: nothing was written, so the precondition could
not be met. Use
.I result=not_blank
on the machine channel to distinguish it from a transport failure.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ACCUDISC_DRIVER_DIR
Directory searched for vendor driver modules
.RI ( accudisc\-drv\- NAME .so )
when
.B \-\-drivers\-dir
is not given. If unset, the installed driver directory is used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/sr0
Default optical device.
.TP
.IR <libdir> /accudisc/drivers/
Installed vendor driver modules.
.TP
.I docs/reference/cli\-machine\-interface.md
The authoritative specification of every machine-readable output format and
exit code this page summarises.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check what is in the drive before doing anything else:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc \-\-device /dev/sr0 disc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Full capture of an audio CD with all its metadata, in one spin-up:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc read \-\-sub raw \-\-pcm disc.pcm \-\-c2f disc.c2 \\
    \-\-subf disc.sub \-\-fulltoc disc.toc.bin \-\-cdtext disc.cdt \-\-map
.fi
.RE
.PP
Test a recovery strategy against a known-damaged span:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc read \-\-start 128000 \-\-count 4000 \-\-verify 3 \\
    \-\-c2\-retries 8 \-\-overlap 4 \-\-ladder 16,8,4 \-\-pcm span.pcm
.fi
.RE
.PP
Characterise an unknown drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc features \-\-all
accudisc speeds \-\-ladder 40,32,24,16,8,4
accudisc c2lag \-\-start 100000 \-\-count 20000 \-\-speed 32
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a rip live from another process:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc read \-\-map\-file status.map \-\-progress\-fd 3 \-q \\
    \-\-pcm disc.pcm 3>progress.fifo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Hardware error census with the vendor driver:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc \-\-driver auto cxscan \-\-speed 8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Burn an audio session, simulating first:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
accudisc write \-\-toc disc.toc \-\-bin disc.bin \-\-simulate
accudisc write \-\-toc disc.toc \-\-bin disc.bin \-\-cdtext disc.cdt
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Setting a read-speed ceiling through SET STREAMING needs
.BR CAP_SYS_RAWIO ;
without it the library falls back to the unprivileged CDROM_SELECT_SPEED path,
which the drive treats as advisory.
.PP
.B \-\-speed
is never auto-restored between invocations, and neither is a bare
.B speed
.IR X .
The vendor read-speed uncap is persistent drive state and is only scoped to one
operation by
.BR "read \-\-uncap" .
.SH CREDITS
AccuDisc's code is original, but several kinds of
.I knowledge
in it were learned from prior art. The full record is
.IR docs/reference/ATTRIBUTION.md ;
this is the summary it requires here.
.TP
.B Data
The drive read-offset table holds user-submitted factual offset measurements
from two independent collections: the
.B REDUMP Disc Preservation Project
(<https://redump.org>), via the table shipped with redumper, and the
.B AccurateRip
drive offset list (<http://www.accuraterip.com/driveoffsets.htm>), which also
supplies the per-drive submission count and agreement percentage. Both are
merged on the development cycle; the library itself never uses a network. The ATIP
media catalogue behind
.B media
compiles public ATIP manufacturer codes, informed by the PlexTools Professional
XL catalogue and cross-validated against cdrecord's
.I diskid.c
table.
.TP
.B Techniques and command knowledge
Implementations are original; these projects documented behaviours, command
layouts and hardware quirks AccuDisc relies on \(em
.B redumper
(READ CD CDB layout, DATA_C2_SUB sector ordering, full-TOC session semantics);
.B cdrtools
(readcd/cdrecord) by J\(:org Schilling (cache-defeat reread pattern, mode
page 01 error-recovery handling, and the original documentation of the
Plextor C1/C2/CU scan opcodes);
.B cdrdao
(mode page 2A speed-field offsets, the DAO writing model);
.B libcdio\-paranoia
(verification and reread strategy background); and
.BR BinaryObjectScanner " and " DRML
by Matt Nadareski, consulted for documented facts about commercial CD-DA
copy-protection schemes and the pressings carrying them.
.TP
.B Vendor drivers
.B QPxTool
(<https://qpxtool.sourceforge.io>, GPL-2.0) is a reference where the Plextor
vendor command set is documented \(em 0xE9 MODE pages, GET/SET direction bits,
per-feature CDB framing, and the SpeedRead and error-scan commands. Those
commands are functional hardware identifiers, no QPxTool source is copied, and
every command was independently verified by raw SG_IO on the maintainer's own
PX-716A. QPxTool is credited as a courtesy reference; the driver is MIT.
